            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indo-Pacific Maritime Continent (MC)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Deforestation in the Indo-Pacific Maritime Continent (MC) has intensified in recent decades, yet its remote impacts on Asian monsoon precipitation remain inadequately understood. Using idealized land-use perturbation experiments with the Community Earth System Model, we demonstrate that MC deforestation reorganizes early-summer (MayJune) precipitation across Asia through large-scale circulation changes.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Deforestation",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "subtropical East Asian coastal zone (SEAC)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "To demonstrate how MC deforestation affects atmospheric circulation and precipitation dynamics, we analyzed the differences between the DEF and CTL simulation runs in terms of y850, low-level winds, and precipitation in MJ (Figs. 1d-e). As shown in Fig. 1d, the decrease in precipitation over SEAC (red box area in Fig. 1a) is associated with the emergence of a low-level atmospheric anticyclonic anomaly, implying enhanced subsidence and a more stable large-scale dynamical environment that suppresses vertical motion and precipitation (Huang et al. 2022).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Deforestation",
            "source_entity_type": "Natural_Hazard",
            "relation": "AFFECTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Peninsula",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Notably, following MC deforestation, an intensification of low-level northeasterly flow is observed over the South China Sea (Fig. 1e), which indicates a weakening of the southwesterly monsoonal flow (Fig. 1c). This intensification of low-level easterly flow has extended from the SEAC region to the north of Bay of Bengal, where it meets the enhanced low-level westerly flow from the Arabian Sea and then induces enhanced wind convergence and local precipitation over the Indian Peninsula.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "land-use-driven teleconnection",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Asia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Collectively, these results identify a land-use-driven teleconnection through which localized deforestation in the MC remotely modulates regional circulation and Meiyu precipitation, highlighting the central role of tropical land-atmosphere coupling in shaping Asian hydroclimate variability.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "land-use-driven teleconnection",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "subtropical East Asian coastal zone (SEAC)",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Our idealized experiments demonstrate that atmospheric circulation changes induced by localized deforestation over the MC can significantly affect early summer precipitation across Asia through a coherent inter-regional teleconnection pathway. Surface warming over the MC enhances local ascent and modifies low-level moisture availability, leading to changes in column-integrated moisture and precipitation characteristics. The resulting large-scale circulation adjustment induces subsidence over SEAC and the tropical Indian Ocean, accompanied by reduced low-level specific humidity and weakened moisture convergence, thereby suppressing Meiyu precipitation over SEAC.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "land-use-driven teleconnection",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Peninsula",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "At the same time, these subsidence anomalies strengthen low-level easterlies from the western Pacific and southerlies from the Indian Ocean, whose convergence promotes enhanced ascent and moisture convergence over the Indian Peninsula, leading to increased precipitation there. These causal linkages are summarized in the schematic diagram (Fig. 7).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
